    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, our lineup leans heavily on high-ceiling platoon advantages against mid-tier arms. **Mickey Moniak** (75.75) and **Vinnie Pasquantino** (75.01) anchor the squad, both benefiting from ideal matchups against Brady Singer and Michael King, respectively. **Mickey Moniak** is in a prime spot to exploit the favorable park factors and the +14% boost provided by his platoon alignment, while **Vinnie Pasquantino** remains a set-it-and-forget-it start against a pitcher with an xERA of 4.49. We are also banking on **Seiya Suzuki** (73.27) to leverage Bailey Ober\u2019s slower delivery to boost his stolen base potential, turning a standard matchup into an elite daily efficiency play.\n\nThe most difficult roster decision was leaving **Josh Naylor** (59.78) on the bench. Despite a solid projection, he narrowly missed the cut due to park-factor headwinds and a tight squeeze at the corner infield spots. While **Josh Naylor** offers excellent counting stat potential, the optimizer prioritized the higher ceiling of **Kyle Karros** (60.20) in the 3B slot, who gains significant leverage from park conditions and SP skill matchups. We are keeping a close watch on **Austin Riley** (67.59); he possesses a strong score, but the 32% rain risk in his contest against Cal Quantrill adds a layer of volatility that could necessitate a late swap if lineups are delayed or weather worsens. Otherwise, we are confident in the speed-first profile of **James Wood** (67.30), whose elite xwOBA of 0.437 makes him an essential fixture in our leadoff spot regardless of the matchup against Gage Jump.",
